        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 901,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 706,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Applies the normalised sinc function, sin(pi x) / (pi x), to every element of a tensor.\n\nIt is the impulse response of an ideal low-pass filter, which makes it the kernel for resampling and band-limited interpolation (usually with a window over it).\n\nTHE CURVE:\n1 at x = 0, symmetric, with ripples that shrink like 1/x on either side. It crosses zero at every nonzero whole number - 1, 2, 3 and -1, -2, -3 - because this is the NORMALISED sinc, with pi inside.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.special.sinc\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nA tensor - or a number or a list, which is turned into one. Receiving it triggers the node. Each element is worked on separately, so the output is the same shape as the input. Whole-number input comes out as floats.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntensor out:\nThe result, element by element, in the same shape. On the same device as the input; float32 in gives float32 out.\n\nWHERE IT IS DEFINED:\nDefined for every real number; at exactly 0 it gives 1, not the 0/0 a hand-written version would.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe metro re-clicks t.linspace ten times a second. t.linspace makes 200 evenly spaced values from its start to its stop (-8 to 8 here), and the upper plot draws them: a straight ramp. The lower plot is sinc of each of those 200 values, so it is the graph of the function from -8 to 8, drawn from -0.3 (bottom) to 1.05 (top). Drag start and stop on t.linspace to look at another stretch.\n\nRELATED:\nt.special.spherical_bessel_j0 is the UNnormalised sinc, sin(x) / x:\nthe same curve stretched by pi. A window such as t.window.kaiser or t.window.hann is what tames sinc's long ripples in practice.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 690,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Applies the normalised sinc function, sin(pi x) / (pi x), to every element of a tensor.\n\nIt is the impulse response of an ideal low-pass filter, which makes it the kernel for resampling and band-limited interpolation (usually with a window over it).\n\nTHE CURVE:\n1 at x = 0, symmetric, with ripples that shrink like 1/x on either side. It crosses zero at every nonzero whole number - 1, 2, 3 and -1, -2, -3 - because this is the NORMALISED sinc, with pi inside.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.special.sinc\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nA tensor - or a number or a list, which is turned into one. Receiving it triggers the node. Each element is worked on separately, so the output is the same shape as the input. Whole-number input comes out as floats.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntensor out:\nThe result, element by element, in the same shape. On the same device as the input; float32 in gives float32 out.\n\nWHERE IT IS DEFINED:\nDefined for every real number; at exactly 0 it gives 1, not the 0/0 a hand-written version would.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe metro re-clicks t.linspace ten times a second. t.linspace makes 200 evenly spaced values from its start to its stop (-8 to 8 here), and the upper plot draws them: a straight ramp. The lower plot is sinc of each of those 200 values, so it is the graph of the function from -8 to 8, drawn from -0.3 (bottom) to 1.05 (top). Drag start and stop on t.linspace to look at another stretch.\n\nRELATED:\nt.special.spherical_bessel_j0 is the UNnormalised sinc, sin(x) / x:\nthe same curve stretched by pi. A window such as t.window.kaiser or t.window.hann is what tames sinc's long ripples in practice."
        },
